 2. 🏗️ Senate passes build-faster bill 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Shoshana Gordon/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The Senate passed a sweeping, bipartisan bill last night designed to cut permitting timelines for energy and infrastructure projects, Axios' Ben Geman reports .

 The 62-33 vote is the first major action by Congress since midterms. Why it matters: The eyes of the entire energy industry are on this bill. If it becomes law, it could transform how America builds energy infrastructure, with major implications for everything from electricity costs to data centers.

 Getting rid of bureaucratic hurdles and creating a more predictable process for energy projects is a top priority for the fossil fuels and renewables sectors. ☀️ Between the lines: The bill also highlights a dilemma for environmentalists — building the infrastructure needed to fight climate change can threaten the very nature they're trying to protect, Axios' Amy Harder writes .

 Wind and solar farms, for example, can require large expanses of land, potentially disrupting wildlife habitats even while helping reduce emissions. Supporters of the bill say the current system takes too long, delaying clean energy and other infrastructure. Opponents warn speeding things up could come at the expense of wildlife, public lands and tribal rights. What's next: The bill heads to the House. Speaker Mike Johnson told Bloomberg last week that he expects it to pass.

