 3. 🗳️ Religious &quot;nones&quot; take office 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 America's religious "nones" have gained a foothold in Congress: Five lawmakers will claim no religious affiliation in January, up from a single member just two years ago, Axios' Russell Contreras reports. 

 Why it matters: Washington remains far more religious than the nation it represents. But the last two election cycles have begun to change that. 🧮 By the numbers: Religiously unaffiliated voters made up 27% of the 2026 midterm electorate, up from 23% in 2024, according to preliminary Fox News voter polling .

 Three lawmakers who claimed no religious affiliation — Reps. Yassamin Ansari (D-Ariz.), Abraham Hamadeh (R-Ariz.) and Emily Randall (D-Wash.) — won reelection in November. They'll be joined in January by Democratic Reps.-elect Chris Rabb of Pennsylvania and Fred Wellman of Missouri — who publicly acknowledged their nonreligious identities before winning their Nov. 3 elections. Zoom out: The nonreligious political bench stretches well beyond Capitol Hill.

 More than 100 openly nonreligious lawmakers are expected to serve in at least 38 state legislatures, up from 81 in 33 states, according to the Center for Freethought Equality . Keep reading . 

 
 
